# Numa tone of voice

Source of truth: `docs/numa-tone-of-voice.md`. Binding on all customer-facing copy (site, email, ads, support) until the user says otherwise.

**Outcome.** A bed that becomes yours, and stays that way. The working word is **discover**. Three statements carry it. None mentions anybody else.

- You do not choose the feel. You discover it.
- It arrives finished.
- It stays right for twenty five years.

**Never mention anyone else.** No competitors, category, "unlike", "most brands", "the industry", comparison tables, or "we do this, they do that." Describe the thing. Stop.

**Never explain why you are good.** State what happens. Do not justify, defend, or pre-empt. Reasons belong on pages built for people who go looking.

**Never write "free".** Use *included* for the standard offer. Use *complimentary* / *with our compliments* for a situational gift. Better: omit money.

**Named services, always capitalised.** Concierge unpacking. Made to Desire. Old mattress removal and recycling. Made to Desire is tailoring: the verb is *make*, never *send* or *replace*.

**Rhythm.** One idea per fragment. Full stops, not commas. Numbers written out in body copy ("twenty five years"); figures in specifications. No hedging.

**Never.** Exclamation marks. "Just", "simply", "easy". Title Case In Buttons And Headings. Scarcity, "hurry", "only £". Emoji. Rhetorical questions in headlines. "Revolutionary", "game-changing", "unique", "premium" about ourselves. Apology in marketing.

**Hierarchy.** Product, then service, then responsibility. Recycling is not a headline.

**Delivery.** Customer's side of the door. Not the staffing.

The test: read it aloud. If it is trying to convince someone, cut it.
